.TH RUN-APPOWNERS 1 "2026-11-03" "cti-agent 1.4" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
run-appowners \- daily: mail each application's scanner report to its owners
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-appowners
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run | \-\-for\-real | \-\-to\-dev )
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent run\-appowners
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run | \-\-for\-real | \-\-to\-dev )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run-appowners
sends each application's Qualys WAS report of open findings, with the scan
account's session removed, to the owner and watchers on that application's
line in
.IR app\-owners.txt ,
the morning after the scan finishes. Many of them are outside the company and
not in Jira, which is why this is email and not a ticket.
.PP
It is run by
.B cti\-agent\-appowners.service
from
.B cti\-agent\-appowners.timer
daily at 07:15
.RB ( Persistent=true ).
With
.B OWNER_ALLOW_DOMAINS
unset it logs that the lane is off and exits 0, except on
.BR \-\-to\-dev .
.PP
The steps, in order:
.TP
.B 1. Plan
.B cti\-appscan \-\-since 48h \-\-deliveries\-out \-\-delivered\-log
lists the scans finished since the last run and not yet sent or held. Fatal
on a non-zero exit.
.TP
.B 2. Prepare
.B cti\-appreport \-\-deliveries \-\-out\-dir
fetches, redacts and writes each email
.RB ( \-\-no\-fetch
on a dry run). Fatal on a non-zero exit.
.TP
.B 3. Send
One
.B cti\-mailer \-\-lane owner \-\-app\-id \-\-app\-name
per application, with the report attached. The mailer, not this runner,
decides the recipients (see
.BR cti\-mailer (1)).
A refused send is logged and tried again on the next run.
.TP
.B 4. Record
On a real run only,
.B cti\-appscan \-\-commit\-delivered
records each sent scan, and each held one, in
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/appowners\-sent.json ,
so each is dealt with once.
.TP
.B 5. Operator
Held scans and watchers refused for their domain go to
.BR cti\-alert (1)
as one HOLD. Never fatal.
.PP
The working directory is deleted on every way out.
.SH HOW TO RUN
\fBrun-appowners\fR mails each application owner their report, so it has no default: it has to be told how to run. Give exactly one of
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR (plan and check every recipient, fetch and send nothing), \fB\-\-for\-real\fR (fetch, redact and send),
or \fB\-\-to\-dev\fR (a test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only; it may be combined with \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR to rehearse the test send).
With none it prints a usage message and exits 2 without doing anything, and
two that contradict each other are refused the same way. This is the rule for
every fleet command that changes something, whether a person, a systemd unit
or the heartbeat runs it, so a unit that lost its flag fails loudly instead of
quietly doing nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Fetch, redact and send. One of this, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR or \fB\-\-to\-dev\fR is required.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Plan and check every recipient through
.BR "cti\-mailer \-\-dry\-run" .
Fetches no report (a report is created in the scanner), sends, records and
alerts nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-to\-dev
Every email, with its report, to
.B FLEET_DEV_EMAIL
only, subject prefixed [DEV TEST]; the operator alert goes there too. Records
nothing, so the real run still sends. Works before
.B OWNER_ALLOW_DOMAINS
is set.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OWNER_ALLOW_DOMAINS
Domains an owner or watcher may be at, comma-separated and exact. Unset: the
lane is off.
.TP
.B APPSCAN_OWNERS_FILE
The owners file; default
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/app\-owners.txt .
.TP
.B FLEET_DEV_EMAIL
Where
.B \-\-to\-dev
sends.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $FLEET_HOME/state/appowners\-sent.json
Last scan sent or held per application.
.TP
.I $FLEET_HOME/state/appowners\-<stamp>/
This run's reports and emails, 0700, deleted when the run ends.
.SH DRY RUNS AND THE LANE LOCK
A dry run and a test send
.RB ( \-\-to\-dev ,
with or without
.BR \-\-dry\-run )
write nothing a real run reads. They work under
.I $FLEET_HOME/dryrun/
(0700), emptied at the start of each one; nothing in the fleet reads it.
Its plan and work directory (a test send's fetched reports included) go in
.IR dryrun/appowners/ ,
and are deleted on every way out as before. It plans from the real
delivered log,
.IR state/appowners\-sent.json ,
and never writes it. The lock matters most here: two real runs at once
would plan the same finished scans and mail every owner twice.
.PP
Each run takes the lane lock,
.IR state/locks/appowners.lock ,
with
.BR flock (2).
A dry run or test send that finds it held \- a timer run, or another
person's rehearsal \- says which process holds it and exits 75 without doing
anything; it never waits and never runs beside the real run. A real run
waits for it up to
.B FLEET_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS
(from
.IR fleet.env ,
default 600, under the unit's
.BR TimeoutStartSec ),
then, if another real run still holds it, fails with exit status 1, so the
unit fails and it can be rerun; if a rehearsal still holds it, goes ahead
with a warning, because a rehearsal writes only its own files. The
kernel drops the lock when the process ends, however it ends, so there is
no stale lock to clear.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done, including when the lane is off, nothing was new, or some sends were
refused (each is logged and retried).
.TP
.B 1
A missing binary, an unknown argument,
.B \-\-to\-dev
without
.BR FLEET_DEV_EMAIL ,
or the plan or preparation step failed.
.TP
.B 75
A dry run or test send refused because another run of the lane holds
the lane lock. Nothing was done; run it again when that run has finished.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo cti\-agent run\-appowners \-\-dry\-run
sudo cti\-agent run\-appowners \-\-to\-dev
journalctl \-u cti\-agent\-appowners \-n 50 \-\-no\-pager
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now cti\-agent\-appowners.timer
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-appscan (1),
.BR cti\-appscan (1),
.BR cti\-appreport (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-alert (1)
